Four-term Republican [[Lindsey Graham]] was [[2020 United States Senate election in South Carolina|reelected in 2020]] with 54.4% of the vote and was seeking a fifth term.<ref name="LindseyGraham">{{cite news |last1=Atkinson |first1=Macon |title=US Sen. Lindsey Graham announces 2026 re-election team and a $15 million fundraising war chest |url=https://www.postandcourier.com/news/lindsey-graham-2026-campaign-senate/article_47600b8e-d1bf-11ef-a268-279c36f26769.html |access-date=January 13, 2025 |work=[[The Post and Courier]] |date=January 13, 2025}}</ref> He won the June 9 Republican primary with 57% of the vote,<ref>{{Cite web |date=June 10, 2026 |title=Sen. Lindsey Graham wins primary over 'America First' challenger |url=https://www.politico.com/live-updates/2026/06/09/congress/graham-wins-south-carolina-senate-primary-00955482 |access-date=June 10, 2026 |website=Politico |language=en}}</ref> but died in office on July 11.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Diamond |first=Dan |date=July 12, 2026 |title=Lindsey Graham, longtime South Carolina senator, dies at 71 |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2026/07/12/lindsey-graham-longtime-south-carolina-senator-dies-71/ |access-date=July 12, 2026 |newspaper=The Washington Post |language=en-US |issn=0190-8286}}</ref> [[Governor of South Carolina|Governor]] [[Henry McMaster]] appointed Graham's sister, [[Darline Graham|Darline]], to serve the remainder of his term. She subsequently sought a full term. In the August 11 special Republican primary, Darline Graham and U.S. Representative [[Ralph Norman]] advanced to an August 25 runoff, which Graham won with 52% of the vote to Norman's 48%.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kinnard |first=Meg |date=August 25, 2026 |title=Sen. Darline Graham wins GOP primary runoff to replace her late brother in US Senate |url=https://apnews.com/article/south-carolina-election-lindsey-graham-03899983c3702842039e99d62e16ddad |access-date=August 25, 2026 |language=en|last2=Collins |first2=Jeffrey}}</ref>

Pediatrician [[Annie Andrews (pediatrician)|Annie Andrews]]<ref>{{cite news |last1=Bustos|first1=Joseph|title=This SC pediatrician ran for Congress in 2022. Now she wants to oust Sen. Graham |url=https://www.thestate.com/news/politics-government/article307292841.html |access-date=May 29, 2025|work=[[The State (newspaper)|The State]] |date=May 29, 2025}}</ref> won the Democratic primary with 61% of the vote.<ref>{{Cite web |date=June 9, 2026 |title=Democrat Annie Andrews to face off against Sen. Lindsey Graham in South Carolina, CBS News projects |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/south-carolina-senate-annie-andrews-lindsey-graham-democratic-primary-results/ |access-date=June 10, 2026 |publisher=CBS News |language=en-US}}</ref>

[[South Carolina Libertarian Party]] nominee Kasie Whitener and [[Constitution Party (United States)|Constitution Party]] nominee Mark Hackett were nominated by convention.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Bassett|first=Marley |date=March 25, 2026 |title=LIST: 2026 primary elections in the Midlands |url=https://www.wistv.com/2026/03/25/list-2026-primary-elections-midlands/ |access-date=March 30, 2026|publisher=WIS-TV |language=en-US
}}</ref> 2022 US Senate candidate [[Catherine Fleming Bruce]] is running as a write-in candidate.
