The '''2026 United States Senate election in Kansas''' will be held on November 3, 2026, to elect a member of the [[United States Senate]] to represent the state of [[Kansas]]. 

[[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] incumbent [[Roger Marshall]] is seeking a second term. He is being challenged by [[Adam Hamilton (pastor)|Adam Hamilton]], senior [[pastor]] of the [[United Methodist Church of the Resurrection]]. In the August 4 [[primary elections]], Marshall won the Republican nomination against minimal opposition. Hamilton won the Democratic nomination in a heavily divided field of candidates.

Democrats have not won a Kansas Senate election since [[1932 United States Senate election in Kansas|1932]].

== Background ==
Though generally considered to be a [[red state]],<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.newsweek.com/roger-marshall-speaks-out-against-ice-as-gop-fears-it-could-lose-kansas-12481531 |title=Roger Marshall Speaks Out Against ICE as GOP Fears It Could Lose Kansas |last=Rahman |first=Billal |work=[[Newsweek]] |date=September 24, 2026 |access-date=September 24, 2026}}</ref><ref name="NYT">{{Cite news |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/14/us/politics/jd-vance-kansas-senate-race.html |title=Vance Campaigns in Kansas as Democrats Target a Republican Stronghold |last=Bidgood |first=Jess |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=September 14, 2026 |access-date=September 24, 2026}}</ref> Kansas has shown greater competitiveness over time due to shifts in the traditionally [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]]-leaning [[Kansas City metropolitan area|Kansas City suburbs]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Glueck |first=Katie |date=October 5, 2026 |title=Pastor's Senate Primary Win Gives Democrats a Ray of Hope in Deep-Red Kansas |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/04/us/politics/hamilton-marshall-kansas-senate-election.html |access-date=October 9, 2026 |work=[[The New York Times]] |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> While Republicans control both houses of the [[Kansas Legislature]],<ref>{{Cite web |title=What happened to GOP supermajorities? Kansas Republicans appear to win big |url=https://www.cjonline.com/story/news/politics/elections/2024/11/06/kansas-republicans-appear-to-win-big-grow-legislative-supermajorities/76085263007/ |last=Alatidd |first=Jason |work=[[The Topeka Capital-Journal]] |language=en-US |date=November 6, 2024 |access-date=September 24, 2026}}</ref> [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democrats]] control the [[Governor of Kansas|governorship of Kansas]], which they flipped from Republican control in [[2018 Kansas gubernatorial election|2018]], and held in [[2022 Kansas gubernatorial election|2022]].<ref name="NYT"/> Kansas has not elected a Democrat to the U.S. Senate since [[1932 United States Senate election in Kansas|1932]].<ref name="NYT"/> Incumbent senator [[Roger Marshall]] was [[2020 United States Senate election in Kansas|elected in 2020]] with 53.2% of the vote, winning by 11.4% despite expectations of a close race.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Kansas 2020 Senate election results |url=https://www.cnn.com/election/2020/results/state/kansas/senate |website=[[CNN]] |language=en |access-date=September 24, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Marshall keeps Kansas Senate seat in Republican hands |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2020/11/03/kansas-senate-election-results-2020-433577 |last=Bustillo |first=Ximena |website=[[Politico]] |date=November 3, 2020 |access-date=September 24, 2026}}</ref>

