== Third-party and independent candidates ==
=== Nominee ===
*David C. Graham, attorney ([[Libertarian Party (United States)|Libertarian]])<ref>{{cite web|title=Candidates for the 2026 General|url=https://sos.ks.gov/elections/elections_upcoming_candidate.aspx|publisher=Kansas Secretary of State|access-date=September 18, 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260501151638/https://www.sos.ks.gov/elections/elections_upcoming_candidate.aspx|archive-date=May 1, 2026|url-status=dead}}</ref>

== General election ==
An issue raised during the campaign was Roger Marshall's history of suing over 700 of his patients for unpaid bills while he worked as an [[OB/GYN]] in Kansas. ''[[The New York Times]]'' reported that in at least 81 cases, patients were arrested at the request of Marshall's lawyers for failure to appear in court for their unpaid debts. There were 13 additional cases where an arrest warrant was requested, but the outcome is unknown. ''The New York Times'' additionally interviewed several of the patients who were sued by Marshall, most of whom said they lived paycheck-to-paycheck and did not have the money to pay the bills.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Kliff |first=Sarah |date=2026-09-08 |title=A Doctor Sued 700 Patients for Debts; 81 Were Arrested. Now He's a Senator. |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/09/08/us/politics/roger-marshall-obgyn-doctor-debts.html |access-date=2026-09-29 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> Hamilton produced an advertisement featuring a woman who said she was arrested for missing a $50 payment to Marshall. The Marshall campaign sent a letter alleging the ad was "defamatory" because the woman was arrested for failing to appear in court over larger debts to him, rather than for missing one bill, and that she was subject to several other court proceedings.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kaminski |first=Anna |date=2026-09-28 |title=Roger Marshall alleges defamation in new ad about former Kansas patient arrested while in debt • Kansas Reflector |url=https://kansasreflector.com/2026/09/28/roger-marshall-alleges-defamation-in-new-ad-about-former-kansas-patient-arrested-while-in-debt/ |access-date=2026-09-29 |website=Kansas Reflector |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Hudnall |first=David |date=September 29, 2026 |title=With new defamation threat, Roger Marshall is making a bad story worse |url=https://www.kansascity.com/opinion/article317419376.html |work=Kansas City Star}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Alatidd |first=Jason |title=What to know about Roger Marshall patient featured in Adam Hamilton ad |url=https://www.cjonline.com/story/news/politics/elections/congressional/2026/09/29/roger-marshall-disputes-adam-hamilton-political-ad-featuring-patient/91986984007/ |access-date=2026-09-29 |website=The Topeka Capital-Journal |language=en-US}}</ref>

The election received national attention for the use of an [[Antisemitic trope|antisemitic slur]] by President [[Donald Trump]] during a Marshall campaign rally in order to deflect allegations about the issue.<ref name="Website-2">{{Cite web |last=Hills |first=Grace |date=October 20, 2026 |title=Trump used antisemitic slur in Kansas while defending Marshall's lawsuits against patients |url=https://www.kcur.org/politics-elections-and-government/2026-10-20/trump-used-antisemitic-slur-in-kansas-while-defending-marshalls-lawsuits-against-patients |access-date=October 21, 2026 |website=KCUR - Kansas City news and NPR |language=en}}</ref><ref name="News-1">{{Cite news |last=Gorelick |first=Evan |date=October 20, 2026 |title=Trump Invokes Antisemitic Trope Again at Rally for Kansas Senator |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/10/20/us/trump-antisemitic-trope-kansas-rally-marshall.html |access-date=October 21, 2026 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref name="Website-3">{{Cite web |date=October 20, 2026 |title=Trump Uses Antisemitic Slur to Defend GOP Senator in Combative Speech |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-10-20/trump-uses-slur-to-defend-gop-senator-in-combative-rally-speech |access-date=October 21, 2026 |website=Bloomberg News}}</ref><ref name="Website-1">{{Cite web |last=Hartmann |first=Margaret |date=October 20, 2026 |title=Trump Helps Out by Dropping Antisemitic Slur at Kansas Rally |url=https://nymag.com/intelligencer/article/trump-antisemitic-slur-shylock-kansas-rally.html |access-date=October 21, 2026 |website=Intelligencer |language=en}}</ref> Former Republican representative [[Barbara Comstock]] said that "Trump once again lets loose his anti-Semitic rhetoric" in the incident.<ref name="News-2" /> The [[White House]] did not respond to a request for comment about the President's use of "[[shylock]]".<ref name="News-2" /> It was not the first time that Trump had used the term in the campaign trail, and he had also received criticism for its use in 2025, when he had claimed that he was unaware that the term was an antisemitic slur against Jews.<ref name="News-2">{{Cite news |title=Trump criticized for again using antisemitic term 'Shylock' during speech at a rally |url=https://www.timesofisrael.com/liveblog_entry/trump-criticized-for-again-using-term-shylock-during-speech-at-a-rally/ |access-date=October 21, 2026 |work=The Times of Israel |language=en-US |issn=0040-7909}}</ref> A statement denouncing the incident, though not denouncing Trump by name was released by the [[Anti-Defamation League]].<ref name="Website-4">{{Cite web |last=Valdez |first=Ana |date=October 21, 2026 |title=Jewish community responds after President Trump uses antisemitic slur at Wichita rally |url=https://www.kwch.com/2026/10/21/jewish-community-responds-after-president-trump-uses-antisemitic-slur-wichita-rally/ |access-date=October 21, 2026 |website=KWCH |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Mackey |first=Robert |date=2026-10-20 |title=ADL condemns Trump's antisemitic slur but fails to mention him by name |url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/oct/20/trump-kansas-rally-antisemitic-slur-anti-defamation-league |access-date=October 21, 2026 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref>

[[The Kansas City Star]] described the Marshall and Hamilton campaigns as making several exaggerated or false claims regarding [[Jeffrey Epstein]], the [[Epstein files]], and a case of [[sexual abuse]] at Church of the Resurrection.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hudnall |first=David |date=October 14, 2026 |title=Fact check: Marshall TV ad accusing Hamilton of protecting child sexual predator |url=https://www.kansascity.com/opinion/article317547018.html |access-date=October 21, 2026 |website=The Kansas City Star}}</ref>

