==== September 12 ====
The first debate was [[Informal wear|informal]], and held at the [[Kansas State Fair]].<ref name="Website-6">{{Cite web |last=Rankin |first=Jason Alatidd and Charles |title=Roger Marshall and Adam Hamilton debate with raucous state fair crowd |url=https://www.cjonline.com/story/news/politics/elections/congressional/2026/09/12/roger-marshall-and-adam-hamilton-debate-at-kansas-state-fair/91717947007/ |access-date=2026-10-22 |website=The Topeka Capital-Journal |language=en-US}}</ref> 

It was marked by a "raucous" and "vocal" crowd "that was far more loudly opposed to the incumbent U.S. senator."<ref name="Website-6" /> 

==== October 21 ====
FOX4 hosted the October debate. Economic issues, including [[Gasoline and diesel usage and pricing|gas prices]], [[inflation]], [[Tariffs in the second Trump administration|tariffs]], and the [[United States affordability crisis|American affordability crisis]] were the leading topics of the debate.<ref name="Website-5">{{Cite web |last=Koranda |first=Stephen |date=2026-10-22 |title=Marshall and Hamilton clash at last debate |url=https://www.kcur.org/politics-elections-and-government/2026-10-21/control-of-the-senate-goes-through-kansas-marshall-and-hamilton-clash-at-last-debate |access-date=2026-10-22 |website=KCUR - Kansas City news and NPR |language=en}}</ref> Marshall called for maintaining [[Tariffs in the second Trump administration|high taxes on imports]] and other [[Tariffs in the second Trump administration|tariffs]], while Hamilton argued that they were a form of [[Regressive tax|taxation]] that "functioned as an unpredictable tax on Kansans".<ref name="Website-5" /> 

Foreign policy was also a central issue in the debate. Marshall supported [[2026 Iran war|continued operations in the Iran War]], while Hamilton called for an exit as soon as it was sufficiently possible.<ref name="Website-5" /> Marshall defended Trump's agreement to get diesel from and his attempts to [[Russia–United States relations|geopolitically reapproach Russia]], while Hamilton claimed it would be "selling out our allies, the [[Ukrainians]]" in the [[Russo-Ukrainian war (2022–present)|current Russo-Ukrainian war]].<ref name="Website-5" /> Marshall referred to Israel as "one of America's greatest allies".<ref name="Website-5" /> 

Marshall expressed support for a "light touch" on [[Regulation of artificial intelligence|AI regulation]] and said that America must achieve [[superintelligence]] in [[Artificial intelligence arms race|the arms race]] before China.<ref name="Website-5" /> Hamilton argued that [[progress in artificial intelligence]] poses an "[[Existential risk from artificial intelligence|potential existential threat to the human race]]" and that "we can’t just go with a light touch and hope everybody does their very best".<ref name="Website-5" />

